\section{Introduction}

\subsection{Background}
Modern hydraulic infrastructure---including pipelines, water distribution networks, and industrial plants---depends on continuous monitoring of fluid states such as pressure, flow rate, and turbulence. Conventional telemetry systems are vulnerable to data tampering, model drift, and a lack of physical grounding in their security mechanisms.

\subsection{Motivation}
There is a growing need for telemetry systems that not only measure physical states but also provide cryptographically verifiable guarantees of integrity and authenticity. KCFM addresses this need by fusing physical law, numerical simulation, and cryptography into a single coherent pipeline.

\subsection{Limitations of Existing Systems}
Existing systems typically:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Rely on unsecured or weakly secured data channels.
  \item Treat simulation and cryptography as independent layers.
  \item Lack strict physical invariants to validate numerical correctness.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{KCFM Concept Overview}
KCFM introduces a cryptophysical engine in which:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Fluid dynamics solvers generate physically consistent state fields.
  \item These states are discretized into tensors and flattened into vectors.
  \item Kinetic entropy is computed from the flow field and used to derive cryptographic keys.
  \item Authenticated encryption seals each state snapshot into an immutable record.
\end{itemize}

