\section{Theoretical Foundations}

\subsection{Governing Fluid Dynamics}

The core hydraulic behavior is modeled using the Navier--Stokes equations for incompressible flow:
\begin{equation}
  \rho\left(\frac{\partial \bm{u}}{\partial t} + \bm{u}\cdot\nabla\bm{u}\right)
  = -\nabla p + \mu\nabla^2\bm{u} + \bm{f},
\end{equation}
where $\rho$ is fluid density, $\bm{u} = (u,v,w)$ is the velocity field, $p$ is pressure, $\mu$ is dynamic viscosity, and $\bm{f}$ represents body forces.

For porous or boundary-filtered segments, Darcy's law is used:
\begin{equation}
  Q = -\frac{kA}{\mu}\frac{dp}{dx},
\end{equation}
where $Q$ is volumetric flow rate, $k$ is permeability, $A$ is cross-sectional area, and $\frac{dp}{dx}$ is the pressure gradient.

\subsection{State Tensor Fieldization}

At each timestep $t$, the fluid state is discretized over a spatial grid into a tensor:
\begin{equation}
  T_{i,j,k}(t) = [p, u, v, w, \eta]_{i,j,k}(t),
\end{equation}
where $\eta$ denotes a turbulence or dissipation scalar.

This tensor is flattened into a state vector:
\begin{equation}
  \bm{S}(t) = \text{flatten}\big(T(t)\big),
\end{equation}
which encodes the complete hydraulic state at time $t$.

\subsection{Kinetic Entropy}

A kinetic intensity or entropy measure is defined as:
\begin{equation}
  H_{\text{kinetic}}(t) = \int_V \left\|\nabla \bm{u}(t)\right\|^2 \, dV,
\end{equation}
where $V$ is the domain volume. This quantity captures the spatial variability and intensity of the flow field.

\subsection{Key Generation}

A cryptographic key is derived from the kinetic entropy and the state vector using a key derivation function (e.g., HKDF):
\begin{equation}
  K(t) = \text{HKDF}\big(H_{\text{kinetic}}(t) \parallel \bm{S}(t)\big),
\end{equation}
where $\parallel$ denotes concatenation. This couples physical dynamics directly into the cryptographic key space.

\subsection{Cryptographic Layer}

Authenticated encryption is applied to the state vector:
\begin{equation}
  C(t) = \text{Encrypt}\big(\bm{S}(t), K(t)\big),
\end{equation}
using AES-GCM-SIV for misuse-resistant AEAD, with optional lattice-based extensions for post-quantum security.

\section{System Architecture}

\subsection{Pipeline Overview}

The KCFM pipeline consists of the following stages:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Physical solver: Navier--Stokes and Darcy-based computation.
  \item State tensorization: construction of $T(t)$ from $[p,u,v,w,\eta]$.
  \item Vectorization: flattening $T(t)$ into $\bm{S}(t)$.
  \item Entropy computation: evaluation of $H_{\text{kinetic}}(t)$.
  \item Key derivation: generation of $K(t)$.
  \item Encryption: production of ciphertext $C(t)$.
  \item Verification: physical and cryptographic integrity checks.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Module Breakdown}

\paragraph{Physical Solver}
Solves the governing PDEs under specified boundary conditions, including transient events such as motor hammer phenomena.

\paragraph{Tensorizer}
Maps scalar and vector fields into a structured tensor representation $T(t)$.

\paragraph{Entropy Engine}
Computes $H_{\text{kinetic}}(t)$ from the velocity field gradients.

\paragraph{Key Generator}
Applies HKDF or similar KDF to produce $K(t)$ from physical and numerical inputs.

\paragraph{Encryption Mesh}
Implements AES-GCM-SIV and optional lattice-based schemes to encrypt $\bm{S}(t)$.

\paragraph{Verification Layer}
Performs mass conservation checks and cryptographic integrity audits.

